Porous flow, Darcy flux and the scale of a permeability (@fd-porous: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @fluid-dynamics, #source "../sources/Advanced-Fluid-Dynamics-Mei-MIT.lingua#r_21NXVH78F0NYDPDVTG66PG261P", #source "../sources/Fundamentals-of-Multiphase-Flow-Brennen.lingua#r_0T6RH2YC70Z23SR3H4QZDJ69JM", #source "../sources/Elementary-Fluid-Dynamics-McDonough.lingua#r_7F9FTDV2VV4P28PG16J5PJSK18", #fluid-dynamics "../vortical/Vorticity-transport-stretching-diffusion-and-baroclinicity.lingua#r_0T941GM8MCPAABF9AFXBV21TJZ") { r_6F57YVHJSPPTDA0FGV79KTX1QY
**Read:** [Advanced fluid dynamics — Mei notes and MIT modules](../sources/Advanced-Fluid-Dynamics-Mei-MIT.lingua#r_21NXVH78F0NYDPDVTG66PG261P), chapter 6 Darcy basis, homogenization and instability; [Fundamentals of Multiphase Flow — Christopher E. Brennen](../sources/Fundamentals-of-Multiphase-Flow-Brennen.lingua#r_0T6RH2YC70Z23SR3H4QZDJ69JM), porous and dispersed-flow modelling where applicable; [Lectures in Elementary Fluid Dynamics — James M. McDonough](../sources/Elementary-Fluid-Dynamics-McDonough.lingua#r_7F9FTDV2VV4P28PG16J5PJSK18), scaling and viscous-force balances.

Averaging flow through many connected pores can produce Darcy's law q=−(K/μ)(∇p−ρg). Here q is discharge per total sample area, not generally the mean velocity inside the pore space; K is permeability, potentially a tensor. Its dimensions are length squared. A representative averaging volume must be large enough to sample pore structure yet small relative to resolved field variation.

Derive a one-dimensional saturated pressure drop and distinguish permeability from hydraulic conductivity, which also includes fluid properties and the selected head convention. Compare isotropic and layered media. At larger pore Reynolds number, inertial corrections can matter; at boundaries or in partially saturated flow, additional conditions and state variables are required.

Measure or inspect pressure-flow evidence at several imposed gradients and in both directions where feasible. Separate sample variability, inlet/end effects, saturation history, evolving deposition and fluid viscosity. Fit a law on one range and test another. Use the full homogenization treatment to explain which details the effective law suppresses and which cannot be recovered from one bulk coefficient.

**Continue:** [Vorticity transport, stretching, diffusion and baroclinicity](../vortical/Vorticity-transport-stretching-diffusion-and-baroclinicity.lingua#r_0T941GM8MCPAABF9AFXBV21TJZ).

